  "query_preview": "..\n\nLong wave troughing across eastern N America will dominate the \nforecast across the Great Lakes region through the weekend. Water \nvapor imagery shows a secondary mid level wave sliding south across \nLake Mi and ern Wisconsin this afternoon. As this wave advances \nsouth this evening, a more northerly flow will become established \nacross Lower Mi. This will advect a little drier low level air into \nSe Mi, offering an opportunity for at least some partial clearing. \nThe latest RRFS and HRRR are strongly supportive in the development \nof a dominate lake effect band from Sturgeon point across the \nnortheastern thumb late this evening and continuing into at least \nearly Sat morning, affecting locations primarily across E Huron and \nNE Sanilac Counties. Modified model soundings for 850mb temps of -5 \nto -6C and lake temps around 10C indicate equilibrium levels of 7 to\n9 kt feet within moist mid level air and enhanced boundary layer \nconvergence. While cold enough to contribute to good enhancement off \nthe Lake, the airmass does not appear cold enough to sufficiently \ncool the boundary layer enough to provide anything more than minor \nsnow accumulations on grassy surfaces within any heavier convective \nelements. While hi res guidance suggests a narrow axis of precip in \nthe .4 to .8 inch range, thermal profiles suggest precip type being \nmainly a rain/melting snow mix. \n\nA broad low level cyclonic flow associated with the long wave \ntroughing will hold across Lower Mi through most of the weekend. A \nbacking of the low level flow toward the north-northwest on Saturday \nwill drive the Lake Michigan moisture plume into Se Mi. Model \nsoundings suggest the moisture becoming trapped under a deep low \nlevel inversion, likely resulting in mostly cloudy skies into the \nday Sunday. The moisture depth will become more shallow and thus \noffer little support for precip. The cloud cover will also limit the \ndiurnal temperature range through the weekend, with daytime highs in \nthe 40s and Sat night lows in the 30s. \n\nStrong high pressure will expand into Lower MI late Sunday/Sunday \nnight, supportive of clearing skies. This high will be associated \nwith a long wave ridge which will become established across the ern \nUS next week. This broad mid level subsidence will result in a \npersistent low level anticylone across the ern US through much of the \nweek. This pattern will lead to dry and seasonally mild condition \nacross the forecast area Monday through the end of the forecast \nperiod.",
